You do not have to keep the layering in your head; the build keeps it for you.
Each layer is its own module with a one-way import table, so a reach across
layers is a compile error. The diagram above is the map, and
[architecture](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)
is the full account.

## Four names that confuse newcomers

| Name | Job |
| --- | --- |
| **Peer** | The caller-owned engine handle. You feed inbound frames, call `step`, drain outbound frames, and read events. It opens no sockets. |
| **Session** | The one-partner application object: `connect` / `listen`, `fetch`, blocking `call`. It owns one live conversation. |
| **Host** | The multi-session CapTP product: one identity, many sessions. It routes introductions between sessions and settles promise waits (Goblins calls a promise a *vow*). Its socket-free core lives in `driver/core`. |
| **Bridge** | The Zig embedder that answers the Host's action queue (dial, flush, close, entropy) over a completion loop. Bindings do the same job through `libpancake_host`. |

Ordinary apps stay on `Session`. An introduction settles as a live capability
inside the call, because every `Session` is backed by a Host. The `Bridge` and
completion-loop pairing is the path when one thread must serve many
connections. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers three-party
introductions.

## The protocol core

The core is synchronous. It holds no sockets and starts no background work, so
you can test it without a host runtime.

- **foundation** owns shared primitives: a `Frame` owns bytes, a `FrameView`
  borrows them, and IDs are plain values.
- **model** is the CapTP vocabulary, independent of the bytes that carried it.
- **codec** turns model values into wire bytes and back.
  [Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) is the Goblins floor;
  [Cap'n Proto](https://capnproto.org/) is the second encoding.
  [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) compares them.
- **session** is the authority: import, export, and answer tables, and what is
  valid. A codec never decides whether a reference is allowed.
- **engine** is the `Peer` above: feed frames, `step`, drain outbound, read
  events. It only dispatches; session owns whether a reference is valid.

## Above the core

- **actors** is a Goblins-shaped `Vat`: near-call `$`, eventual-send `<-`, and
  promises. It adds no protocol semantics the engine lacks.
- **driver/core** is the multi-session serve core with no sockets of its own.
  A build check keeps I/O out of it.
- **net** is where `std.Io` enters. Residents include cleartext `tcp`,
  `keyed-tcp`, `noise-tcp`, `inproc`, optional [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/)
  QUIC, and [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')), among others.
  [Transports]($link.page('transports')) explains what a netlayer is;
  [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) is the decision
  table.

> **Vat**: one single-threaded container of objects, the unit
> [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) and the wider ocap
> world organize around. Every call into a vat runs as one *turn*, start to
> finish, before the next begins.

> **Near-call (`$`) and eventual-send (`<-`)**: from
> [E](http://erights.org/). Near runs now in your vat. Eventual queues a later
> turn and returns a promise. That is also the only way to call across a
> session.

## Surfaces out of the core

The [Surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')) page is the consumer's view.

- **c_abi** is opaque handles, fixed-width codes, `ptr + len`, and explicit
  release. `libpancake_host` is the multi-session host the bindings wrap.
- **driver** is native Zig above `driver/core`: `Session` and `HostRuntime` for
  ordinary native use, plus `Bridge` when one readiness loop must carry many
  connections.

[Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) are that application surface again in Rust,
TypeScript, Python, and Racket, each over the C ABI.
